#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180447} :activity/id #uuid "563e73f4-5c65-5c01-b1d4-1909214d3b23" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050885 :topic/id #uuid "6e3d5713-6e26-51db-8b8f-61d36b28e342" :topic/math-academy-id 3692} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180447 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180700 :tutorial/content "Recall that  if $f(x,y)$ is a function whose partial derivatives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y},\n$$\n\nexist and are continuous at a point $(x_0,y_0) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ then the gradient of $f$ evaluated at $(x_0, y_0)$ is given by\n\n \n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x_0,y_0) = \\dfrac{\\partial f }{\\partial x}(x_0, y_0) \\,\\mathbf{i} + \\dfrac{\\partial f }{\\partial y} (x_0, y_0)\\,\\mathbf{j} \\, .\n$$\n\n \n\nNow suppose that the gradient vector exists at every point $(x,y)$ contained within the set $U\\subset \\mathbb R^2.$ Then, the **gradient vector field** of $f$ is defined as the map\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\longmapsto \\nabla f(x,y), \\qquad (x,y)\\in U.\n$$\n\nAs an example, consider the function $f(x,y)=x^2-y^2.$ To find the gradient vector field of $f,$ we proceed as follows.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =2x\\,i-2y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA sketch of this vector field is shown below.\n\n![](images/74/7437bd110810a0356a25afea897634c152b0441aee8e6403aa051dd2c1f205a7.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "fe62959b-3e46-5af2-ba87-08f376c38b4e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5764 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "42fa31ed-8f97-5aa3-89e8-9d271c417891" :step/math-academy-id 35370 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180452}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180448 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179712 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179574 :question/id #uuid "d83c960d-aeb2-5c31-b26f-f17518220ac0" :question/math-academy-id "e-9444"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "364d2c9a-6e9a-57f8-aa57-c10ee223061b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050027}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407566}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Relationship Between the Vector Field of a Function and Its Contour Plot"} :step/id #uuid "93326d12-a17a-5177-8940-426e5dd661b8" :step/math-academy-id 35372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180452 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179714 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179576 :question/id #uuid "2eaebefb-6b18-5689-a746-0d5dc595991c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9453"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd5c5fd8-409c-5b92-a0f1-4466c76cdbe0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050143}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387256}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient Vector Field of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "cc32145d-a804-5c13-ba20-8531d24745d0" :step/math-academy-id 35387 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180458}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180458 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179719 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179581 :question/id #uuid "d41ebc3c-f998-577e-a50b-a08faadd561f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9474"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a43f82e-6682-56af-994a-b0bdf12b4095" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048917}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424190}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Gradient Vector Field of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "f3f39866-fb9c-5fab-80a0-21104a970e7c" :step/math-academy-id 35460 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180460}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180460 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180705 :tutorial/content "Consider the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = x^2+y,$ shown below. How is this vector field related to the level curves of $f?$\n\n![](images/ef/ef5800723603b78beeb40c4352153f37298d4836905a6d8b751a919924664fa5.png)\n\nTo answer this question, we note the following:\n\n- As we've seen, the gradient of $f$ at a particular point $(x_0, y_0)$ lies *perpendicular* to the level curve $f(x,y) = f(x_0, y_0).$\n\n- Therefore, the gradient field vectors of $f$ at an arbitrary point $(x,y)$ lie perpendicular to its level curve at $(x,y).$\n\n- Moreover, the vectors always point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ is increasing.\n\nNow, if we superimpose the level curves of $f$ onto its vector field, we get a picture that looks as follows:\n\n![](images/5b/5bd2729b82312a50ee0c274fc1591545588f4881e2e279e80aca6b68f76ee824.png)\n\nNotice that the field vectors are indeed perpendicular to the level curves.\n\nWe can use a similar idea to sketch the vector field of a function using its level curves. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "60bb3f88-e435-5258-8b56-9c92481e7976" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5774 :tutorial/title "The Relationship Between the Vector Field of a Function and Its Contour Plot"} :step/id #uuid "7dce8a1b-d2dc-5ba7-9445-52bd966f8de5" :step/math-academy-id 35468 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180448}}] :activity/title "Gradient Vector Fields" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179846}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407570} :activity/id #uuid "9033afb5-8b44-5331-a066-410b7bf87c91" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050885 :topic/id #uuid "6e3d5713-6e26-51db-8b8f-61d36b28e342" :topic/math-academy-id 3692} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407567 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387304 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2280c113-775e-55c4-a01a-374b591fe98d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b8/b8b4cae5128667b4a75097540bddc8dbb853744aee65e96204cf630ccb30fc68.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387306} {:answer/id #uuid "62456e8c-d83a-57a0-89bf-f27036a5952d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e6/e6598c50e72417a0bff5514fa2b8ff9fba7c617cff10dc83e15472259d040846.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387307} {:answer/id #uuid "5d7deb3d-fec1-58c8-a1ac-06e1e23abd55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/41/417f9bd99ad7032ffac7ce258c5122d932b1bb1610237ef030f077ca56815bec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387308} {:answer/id #uuid "4f9c1117-85a8-52e1-8899-dd286a49cfd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8e/8e30bba74fad98713f891950fd052d2ddd2cb2dc349bd9bcb7efe3333b7203dd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387309} {:answer/id #uuid "244086f1-424e-529c-87bd-716006661658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/dc/dcd7de7b52c59ceda71245ef18d7cb1327a0e3f3b93f6f8e0bb8c5e386958217.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387310}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387306} :answer-field/id #uuid "da7d6acf-0c52-544c-acec-75dbce89c666" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "84a12606-a26d-58d9-96bf-2e993f7dda01" :question/math-academy-id "q-122813" :question/problem "![](images/50/50bd80ab9aa2f8d5ac2a453ccfe63815a75379efa7502cf1090eb015264206f6.png)\n\nThe gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be a contour plot of $f(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\nObserve that the given field vectors are perpendicular to the level curves in the following picture.\n\n![](images/b7/b73b05cb7f58bcce1d684c7f19c391ecd2033eb4c47c59daf896546956a9d42f.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/b8/b8b4cae5128667b4a75097540bddc8dbb853744aee65e96204cf630ccb30fc68.png)"} :step/id #uuid "32569fb7-e771-56df-bcee-088a5b89c15c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407569}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407568 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387270 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bec373fa-27cc-5734-871a-ec77749ea6a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/0c/0c425b6fcb88f4c958a7c0371a64a815eda5ebf8ed14b0adc06875de1e24003b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387272} {:answer/id #uuid "6120694d-f5d6-5a30-a9b1-04836470b4fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/af/af7fdf48d36f477174867761b90d9349344029980012f1f7907f544efd3893bc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387273} {:answer/id #uuid "5738a2a6-58f2-5cb8-befa-f76d6d0fb2c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/48/48c57f24904442c74178682db34aaa0113eb84042c78516a4e6f961e73b0f9eb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387274} {:answer/id #uuid "11eee978-2cbc-57da-8a9a-9ffddf6422c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/02/020c3ec7cd0e5e57bb4c6de8516988187c9038d9e5598b116966b71fa8b917d8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387275} {:answer/id #uuid "04551b05-601c-557a-a5a2-69cba4463197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/25/25ed4b4750662433bf4405b5e63ab78396688a985c76e2d34d449c0689830380.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387276}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387275} :answer-field/id #uuid "c39f6b60-6db1-56ec-93e1-e634b438a208" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "14b7ceda-a408-53c9-aeb5-05da06c84fba" :question/math-academy-id "q-122732" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & ={y}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\,i+2xy\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,1),$ $(2,1),$ and $(-2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,1) & ={1}^{2}\\,i+2\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 1\\,j=i-4j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & ={1}^{2}\\,i+2\\cdot 2\\cdot 1\\,j=i+4\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-2,-1) & =(-1)^{2}\\,i+2\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (-1)\\,j=i+4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/02/020c3ec7cd0e5e57bb4c6de8516988187c9038d9e5598b116966b71fa8b917d8.png)"} :step/id #uuid "5422ee42-4513-5e58-91cc-6593fbcca408" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407571}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407569 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407566 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "758d7c9c-7190-58ba-88ca-6ba4209fb9ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/02/02e2b36b593aef6b8477a6e3a546a7a367cd6c7c8de4642684f418222e0d1c96.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407560} {:answer/id #uuid "b0547ab6-4eb4-5605-bedc-e8c09b74d5ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8f/8f79cfa6b73725479bd76db48b212a60b04ed91f3355c9d135e8cf176b3a6b8f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407561} {:answer/id #uuid "b6af969e-293e-5aa6-8efd-61f10b87dbac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/42/426f761b56bbba7549a5d76ecc6340461c3bc76627c9664a46ef4c6063ee7039.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407562} {:answer/id #uuid "e719fb82-e005-592a-9a1d-c08ce892673d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/5f/5f4468465de36289d11b86acb35c38e6d2034ca220d6559ec93b5085d7437d5c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407563} {:answer/id #uuid "eeddcc87-ac3b-50d2-be15-6abdd930a045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/5a/5a05d0be8d775cfa97ae507da2ce7904a11b0f7c8df14d51f2e95f17925f64f7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407564}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407564} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cef7ae1-6ba6-5fd3-82d2-e1546e8de597" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407565}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8917ffe4-7713-5b06-82b5-c913e5f2e407" :question/math-academy-id "q-122880" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=6x+2y?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=-1,$ we get $6x+2y=-1,$ which gives the line $y=-3x-\\frac{1}{2}$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get $6x+2y=0,$ which gives the line $y=-3x$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=2,$ we get $6x+2y=2,$ which gives the line $y=-3x+1$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](images/d6/d6c45af410df1ae974062b777b856b3960e72ad13e14d37a48e5a92cebf74cd1.png)\n\n \n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as both $x$ and  $y$ increase. \n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](images/3a/3aa601a2bb31bb8c009f554361a79135daf1d7abd2779eaf1ae8b2431a01eafa.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](images/5a/5a05d0be8d775cfa97ae507da2ce7904a11b0f7c8df14d51f2e95f17925f64f7.png)"} :step/id #uuid "bae2befe-109e-5175-92c1-22c16c99cc0c"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407570 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256697 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f1e18b0-1117-563b-8441-3490065e61e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d3/d3fc823cfcea400219e553f9e6911a312f0a14c7f39aafb49d1af3463cb3e46b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253939} {:answer/id #uuid "46e3f6a4-7353-56bc-8961-cc92fd971024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/0e/0ea540eb4a48d1fb1777e2d6397e6d38937e9795c4d810b48c0fda0801d1c9ba.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254269} {:answer/id #uuid "64b24222-51a7-5272-9ca7-2db93a07e9b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ba/ba42a974a78df2c0d228e8f5ae543019841c8a7b47e38f02e139fe2d97b8ecf5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254572} {:answer/id #uuid "8ad5cb04-3d21-55c7-a532-d1bd2ca52b4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f4/f4f0b2e96fa7ee0f1dca7d57a2815e93d77665c0285fa81f2a8b2c5965a39415.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254895} {:answer/id #uuid "bde88cfd-9cb5-56e6-ba40-080493eb0ad6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/36/369ebf19405768bcca19f4981f0237647836250548b0a33f9b4fdba92d85ddc9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255365}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255365} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e297b46-c5d5-5311-943f-eb3962e376bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3f30aab0-000a-5430-bdac-144d62e4296d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122693" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =x \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =x\\,i+y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-1,1),$ $(2,2),$ and $(2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-1,1) & =-\\,i+j \\\\ ∇f(2,2) & =2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =2\\,i+(-1)j=2\\,i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/36/369ebf19405768bcca19f4981f0237647836250548b0a33f9b4fdba92d85ddc9.png)"} :step/id #uuid "e28c3322-ca5c-53ca-be1f-019e457471a4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407568}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407571 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256720 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00678095-4785-5a48-bfd2-04906406b23d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8b/8bd5b7575d1693935df0d25a259767f0dd267fc1153139cd55cebad75e3988fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253671} {:answer/id #uuid "03aaca50-6ba1-5767-8a1d-61289921be5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/4c/4c1a0035b37bc0137dc2ac164c980d538f9362feb3b439e663885f8aa7178124.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253699} {:answer/id #uuid "5936829d-c5a5-5317-93a8-8d046c07d07f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/fa/fa8bf282264f1647644cc15b6aece745718bd17617afad2c2530292bfed10b15.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254452} {:answer/id #uuid "70f6299c-8313-5e6f-a9c7-80abdfba5fd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/70/704a1e0712c0c2512e16ac03f1eeb7ecd55996c822eced54ac510f4719485c42.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254671} {:answer/id #uuid "9bf9db65-02b1-5896-a31b-6345273456e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f9/f9d6304bc9cc6b6e74235c66a7d70b96dbea2db44e9ed82606f6025075b76336.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255043}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253671} :answer-field/id #uuid "a46a2d28-3dee-5856-b9b2-7ac4fb2b706b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f67d0b79-608f-51fc-afe3-9623997cda39" :question/math-academy-id "q-122810" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=-1,$ we get ${x}^{2}-y=-1,$ which gives the parabola $y={x}^{2}+1$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get ${x}^{2}-y=0,$ that is, the parabola $y={x}^{2}$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=2,$ we get ${x}^{2}-y=2,$ that is, the parabola $y={x}^{2}-2$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](images/77/77a54ef31c41e0246e3d337a69c7faa161194058e6a5c672ef24d86866a83faf.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move downward and away from the origin $O.$ \n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](images/4e/4e124d5e77ef9a28002aba9aac40f0b7eff1232dd1c08b6bca10b166469675c7.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n \n\n![](images/8b/8bd5b7575d1693935df0d25a259767f0dd267fc1153139cd55cebad75e3988fd.png)"} :step/id #uuid "f8c7a665-4f1b-5c8a-9481-d2fc3fe2131a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407567}}] :activity/title "Gradient Vector Fields" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407559}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424193} :activity/id #uuid "a15d5bb3-e629-5239-97bb-a068e0cf6a5b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050885 :topic/id #uuid "6e3d5713-6e26-51db-8b8f-61d36b28e342" :topic/math-academy-id 3692} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424191 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424190 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0afc3cbd-80cd-5f79-95d1-b02b58d14af7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ce/ce7690cdb4a4da4841dc7f551b73d379945141964de4f9de00dbe6a39a6e36e8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424184} {:answer/id #uuid "25ed12c7-2e43-5b16-b2e3-173b3c5367b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e2/e239433cc1c62333506d14eac022488f2c6786837f17f20e6a1fbba9f25dbf12.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424185} {:answer/id #uuid "99f6e268-33a9-5e9a-a609-e6ee2c4c38d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f6/f6264905bceebe59d764a88f8566dc5eb21237dd28ae5c2052fbd3c766ba6c14.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424186} {:answer/id #uuid "c5dba748-9027-5a4a-bf33-0feb67920158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/4c/4c5c4b297d1aaaa672628f0621e8ed71fbacedd9f418d7d7068da8cba5847315.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424187} {:answer/id #uuid "cad5833b-0aa5-5948-b9b9-b322bb2fcf7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/96/96851fd456d3b9f0a6af941a255926f35e29bbe778ed05556af3f69145e38ef2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424188}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424188} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9f1a53d-9e00-54ab-b914-f5c8b1d4efa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6d9aa2a3-1ea1-5a5e-9577-ca5789b6206d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122737" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=(x-y)^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-y)^{2}) \\\\  & =2(x-y) \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x-y)^{2}) \\\\  & =-2(x-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =2(x-y)\\,i-2(x-y)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(2,1),$ $(1,2),$ and $(1,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(2,1) & =2\\cdot (2-1)\\,i-2\\cdot (2-1)\\,j=2\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,2) & =2\\cdot (1-2)\\,i-2\\cdot (1-2)\\,j=-2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,-1) & =2\\cdot (1-(-1))\\,i-2\\cdot (1-(-1))\\,j=4\\,i-4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/96/96851fd456d3b9f0a6af941a255926f35e29bbe778ed05556af3f69145e38ef2.png)"} :step/id #uuid "067003af-5c9a-5cbe-8472-bab19e50bc36" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424195}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424192 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179973 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ba90d78-1161-52a4-93c0-d995aaaf7e51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/6c/6cf967e8a5d3bc7b2ac021414875f5221ef52790dfbdd6e214cccc7f0e8be2d2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387299} {:answer/id #uuid "bb982230-23d8-541a-a311-793fcd335e70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/4b/4be9586aaa886ccfe092f93c106a5f7233509c798c9a90ecfda0363173dc7afb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387300} {:answer/id #uuid "6f167c07-25dc-5f9e-956f-2fe0933e7ed6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/95/9593ba00b964194c1d91549fe38a1334a478ffd6b724534c4512a11d19d683aa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387301} {:answer/id #uuid "640a700e-3b7c-59e8-83e6-b88bdc953d51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f8/f814f8c7e6d879cbe25e5108704e29464d0c39859371ee4fac3bfb3e72f90696.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387302} {:answer/id #uuid "09b9b72e-d802-5315-b38d-ee456018cf2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/26/26a5c0123d93231a800456e9f06f6ba7450861838c87d4d1b3ae77dab4cec6cf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387303}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387303} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7fe3fc1-2c7d-5b68-96b7-12857eae01aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fe3ebbef-9b0f-50cb-ab18-6df5f7b1d83c" :question/math-academy-id "q-122789" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{y}^{2}}{4}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{y}^{2}}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{y}^{2}}{4}) \\\\  & =-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}\\,i-y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,2),$ $(2,-3),$ and $(2,1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,2) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot (-2)^{2}\\,i-2\\,j=3\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-3) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {2}^{2}\\,i-(-3)\\,j=3\\,i+3\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {2}^{2}\\,i-\\,j=3\\,i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/26/26a5c0123d93231a800456e9f06f6ba7450861838c87d4d1b3ae77dab4cec6cf.png)"} :step/id #uuid "6dfb476d-54a7-53df-8507-c3f88ca01082" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424194}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424193 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179967 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b73086dc-5509-5507-97a5-c7e3bfba2349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/14/14c453b9fcf4670a2356b730253dd7f411b989abde303f059ebb5a9d559a4eff.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177472} {:answer/id #uuid "c0c2aec3-ab10-5812-a6f0-e32442afa1bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/2c/2c01770dd5222fc43a65dd92381917821c127d39a0055ca464582c829ef8d3fa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177473} {:answer/id #uuid "cad68cfa-0e83-5847-b359-8bce8928018f" :answer/