\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/ce/cecc7f28c20523aac9f153022d64da4233ce995e05df3b3e2886b7fb3b20d089.png)"} :step/id #uuid "99216207-5767-5618-bf28-eb5a98073708"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424195 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256708 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b07ebbb-ae2a-5805-a7b7-426af5bcb497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/13/135122e85da455699e45c1e276152b20fee0ac9d18441eb5620b1aa43654c81b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254314} {:answer/id #uuid "8fca9e4f-70ee-50c3-8e6c-d0cd30d777a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/25/2595c11a1b30fee9d76028010d2c7e608250c0d48ce0191d7da2a5412af6a110.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254930} {:answer/id #uuid "cc2f9076-3f65-544d-b6ed-94bd3f9e2b0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e4/e4f9011d4497ac01495c1dcde81d9ba19e9b48f9d78c36ca6259d3a2465983b7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255484} {:answer/id #uuid "dba6b19f-483e-5557-90ff-71eb95e4575c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/3b/3bf6311586ae40bb490414108bbf232f40e1e727da45bbdbe3123e60c17f6268.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255617} {:answer/id #uuid "e36ece5b-4544-54b9-8906-c7cdc5544ad4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/71/716f99a985d8b4909e76f922fc3bf5b3350f9cb7496ae0a0a429421016a19267.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255684}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254930} :answer-field/id #uuid "fefeda5b-1048-597b-a49a-96c4ffaa9319" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7de89f2b-b3a4-52f5-bd83-f755a3097808" :question/math-academy-id "q-122738" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{(y-2x)^{2}}{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{(y-2x)^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-2(y-2x) \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{(y-2x)^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =y-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =-2(y-2x)\\,i+(y-2x)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(1,1),$ $(1,3),$ and $(-2,-3).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(1,1) & =-2\\cdot (1-2\\cdot 1)\\,i+(1-2\\cdot 1)\\,j=2\\,i-j \\\\ ∇f(1,3) & =-2\\cdot (3-2\\cdot 1)\\,i+(3-2\\cdot 1)\\,j=-2\\,i+j \\\\ ∇f(-2,-3) & =-2\\cdot (-3-2\\cdot (-2))\\,i+(-3-2\\cdot (-2))\\,j=-2\\,i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/25/2595c11a1b30fee9d76028010d2c7e608250c0d48ce0191d7da2a5412af6a110.png)"} :step/id #uuid "fe7b4648-c351-5000-80e4-1e9d858043ac" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424192}}] :activity/title "Gradient Vector Fields" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424183}]}
